+### Plan for Nexerelin's random sector
+
+Nexerelin can start a game in a **random sector**: none of vanilla's hand-made core worlds exist
+(no Jangala, no Corvus, none of vanilla's named characters). With **Nexerelin** in the list
+(Optional or Required), every **Mods** field also has **Sector**: hand-made only, random only, both
+(keep), or automatic.
+
+1. Make Nexerelin **Optional** (see [Work with another mod](#work-with-another-mod)).
+2. Open **Problems**. Content that names vanilla's hand-made markets, systems, planets or people is
+   listed ("names vanilla's hand-made jangala …"). It already runs **only in the hand-made sector**
+   (greyed "uses vanilla's jangala" in its Mods field), and so does what only it uses. A part of a
+   quest that only the hand-made sector reaches (behind a Branch case set to hand-made only)
+   doesn't count.
+3. Pick one of its three buttons:
+   - **Only in the hand-made sector**: keep it that way (the default; the button makes it your
+     choice, so it leaves Problems).
+   - **Use a random target**: the quest gets a random target that picks a market like the place
+     (its faction and size preferred), and its markers, Arrive places, named places, pictures and
+     `$id ==` conditions point at the target, so it works in both sectors. Check the target's
+     criteria in the quest's Variables. Not offered where a contact, a speaker or an encounter
+     names the place.
+   - **Keep (runs in both)**: it stays offered in a random sector. SagaLib still never starts a
+     quest whose contact, markers or Arrive places don't exist, and logs why.
+4. Test both: Play mode and the Test context have a **random sector** switch.
+
+In game, the hand-made-only condition is the plain memory key `!$global.nex_randomSector` in
+rules (it works without SagaLib) and `"sector"` in SagaLib's files (SagaLib skips the item in a
+random sector and writes one line to the log). Without Nexerelin every game counts as hand-made.
+
